msgid "Selecting a gRPC server"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus provides two implementations of the gRPC server: gRPC Java (based on "
"Netty) and Vert.x.\n"
"Both of them support TLS."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One of the advantages of the Vert.x based server is the ability to use a "
"single server to handle HTTP requests and gRPC requests. This is useful if "
"you want to expose both REST and gRPC endpoints on the same port. This is "
"not possible with the gRPC Java server (using a separate server)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To select the gRPC server implementation, set the `quarkus.grpc.server.use-"
"separate-server` property in your `application.properties` file:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We recommend the usage of the Vert.x based gRPC server, as it is more "
"flexible and better integrated in the Quarkus ecosystem."
msgstr ""

msgid "Performance tuning for large messages"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When working with large gRPC messages, you may need to tune the HTTP/2 flow "
"control window size to achieve optimal throughput."
msgstr ""

msgid "Unified server mode (Vert.x-based)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using the unified server mode (`quarkus.grpc.server.use-separate-server="
"false`), you can configure the HTTP/2 connection window size:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus.http.http2-connection-window-size` property sets the HTTP/2 "
"connection window size in bytes. The default value is `-1`, which reuses the "
"initial window size setting (typically 64KB). For large messages, increasing "
"this value can significantly improve throughput."
msgstr ""

msgid "Separate server mode (gRPC Java/Netty-based)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using the separate server mode (`quarkus.grpc.server.use-separate-"
"server=true`), you can configure the flow control window using a "
"`ServerBuilderCustomizer`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Listening on a Random Port"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you don't want to specify a port you can set `quarkus.http.port=0` and "
"`quarkus.http.test-port=0` when using Vert.x\n"
"(`quarkus.grpc.server.use-separate-server=false)` or `quarkus.grpc.server."
"port=0` and `quarkus.grpc.server.test-port=0`\n"
"when using the gRPC server. A random open port will be picked by the OS, and "
"a log message printed in the console."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To retrieve the real port, use the `io.quarkus.grpc.runtime.GrpcServer` API, "
"which can be injected into the application\n"
"code as a CDI Bean:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Or into any kind of test, either as a field or test parameter:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Selecting gRPC clients"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As for the server, Quarkus proposes two alternatives for the gRPC clients: "
"gRPC Java and Vert.x.\n"
"Unlike for the server, you can select the transport for each client:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"While it's not the default, we recommend using the Vert.x based client, as "
"it is more flexible and better integrated in the Quarkus ecosystem.\n"
"It does not change the stubs you can use, as they are generated by the gRPC "
"framework.\n"
"However, it changes the way the client communicates with the server."
msgstr ""

msgid "Client-side flow control window"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When working with large messages, you can configure the flow control window "
"for gRPC clients:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The default flow control window is 1MB. Increasing this value can improve "
"throughput when receiving large messages from the server."
msgstr ""

